Beauty as Threshold: When Does Beauty Become a Burden?

How "Threshold of Beauty" Tests the Physiological Limits of Aesthetic Perception

**Ibrahim Shalabi**  
Conceptual Artist and Founder of the "Post-Perception" Movement

---

## Abstract

This paper examines how the artwork "Threshold of Beauty" (*'Atabat al-Jamāl*) tests the physiological limits of aesthetic perception and explores what this means for aesthetic theory. The artwork, a 10-minute immersive audio-visual installation, presents an escalating proliferation of AI-generated birds on four walls and a ceiling—from a single bird (grace) to hundreds (collapse)—until the originally beautiful experience becomes unbearable, forcing viewers to discover their own threshold of tolerance. Through analysis of the artwork's four-phase structure (grace, joy, saturation, collapse) and its physiological effects on the nervous system, this paper argues that "Threshold of Beauty" challenges classical theories of beauty (which assume that more beauty is always better) and proposes a new aesthetic category: **beauty as threshold**. The paper draws on neuroaesthetics (the study of the neural basis of aesthetic experience) and perceptual psychology to explain how escalating sensory stimulation triggers a cascade of neural responses—from pleasurable processing to sensory overload to aversive withdrawal. The paper concludes that "Threshold of Beauty" demonstrates that beauty is not an absolute value but a **quantity with physiological limits**, and that the aesthetic experience is not merely a matter of taste but a matter of **bodily tolerance**.

**Keywords:** Threshold of Beauty, Neuroaesthetics, Aesthetic Perception, Perceptual Overload, Sensory Processing, Beauty, Post-Perception.

---

## 1. Introduction

### 1.1 The Classical Assumption: More Beauty is Better

From Plato to Kant, from the Renaissance to Modernism, Western aesthetics has operated on a fundamental assumption: **beauty is a positive value**. More beauty is better than less beauty. The more harmonious, the more sublime, the more perfectly proportioned, the greater the aesthetic pleasure.

This assumption has been so deeply embedded in aesthetic discourse that it has rarely been questioned. Why would anyone prefer less beauty? Why would anyone voluntarily leave a beautiful experience?

"Threshold of Beauty" challenges this assumption at its core. It asks: **At what point does beauty become a burden? At what point does aesthetic pleasure turn into perceptual pain? And what does this mean for our understanding of beauty itself?**

### 1.2 Research Question

**How does "Threshold of Beauty" test the physiological limits of aesthetic perception, and what does this mean for aesthetic theory?**

This paper argues that "Threshold of Beauty" reveals that beauty is not an absolute value but a **quantity with physiological limits**. Aesthetic experience is not merely a matter of taste, judgment, or cultural conditioning; it is a matter of **bodily tolerance**.

### 1.3 Methodology

The paper employs:
1. **Artwork analysis** of "Threshold of Beauty" (description and phenomenological analysis)
2. **Neuroaesthetic analysis** of the neural processes involved in escalating sensory stimulation
3. **Comparative analysis** between classical aesthetic theories and the threshold model

