## 7. Implications for Aesthetic Theory

### 7.1 Beyond the Harmony Model

Traditional aesthetics has been dominated by the **harmony model**: beauty is harmony, proportion, balance. "Threshold of Beauty" challenges this model by showing that harmony can become oppressive when repeated excessively.

**The harmony model:** Beauty = proper proportion
**The threshold model:** Beauty = quantity × tolerance

### 7.2 The Relational Turn in Aesthetics

"Threshold of Beauty" exemplifies the **relational turn** in contemporary aesthetics. Beauty is not a property of the object; it is a **relation** between the object and the perceiver's nervous system. The same object (the bird) can be beautiful (at low density) or burdensome (at high density). The difference is not in the object but in the **quantity** of the object and the **perceiver's capacity** to tolerate it.

### 7.3 The Body Reclaims Aesthetics

Classical aesthetics, particularly after Kant, has tended to **disembody** the aesthetic experience. Beauty is a judgment of taste, a feeling of pleasure, a cognitive evaluation. The body is, at best, incidental.

"Threshold of Beauty" **re-embodies** aesthetics. The threshold is not a cognitive judgment; it is a **physiological event**. The body decides when to leave. The body's limits are the limits of the aesthetic experience.

**As the manifesto of Post-Perception declares:**

> "The threshold is not a philosophical concept here. It is a biological reality that the viewer experiences at the moment their capacity to continue collapses."

